ral-election poll (Democratic-sponsored, May) showed Baumgartner +6, in line with fundamentals", "Strong Democratic national environment is the main force keeping this short of Safe R"], "rationale": "Expert ratings, the prediction market, and the incumbent's financial advantages all point the same direction in a district that has not elected a Democrat in three decades. A favorable national environment and a May poll showing a six-point race argue Baumgartner is not untouchable, but the evidence still leaves him clearly favored.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic outside spending or a stronger-than-expected national wave in the final weeks could make this genuinely competitive.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Baumgartner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Washington's 5th District, anchored in Spokane, has not elected a Democrat in three decades and leans Republican by about five points. Incumbent Representative Michael Baumgartner faces Democrat Carmela Conroy in a rematch. Baumgartner holds a large financial advantage, raising about $2.1 million to Conroy's $723,000, with a wide lead in cash on hand.\n\nMost expert raters call the district Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Conroy roughly a one-in-nine chance. The only general-election poll, a Democratic-sponsored survey from May, showed Baumgartner ahead by six points. A national environment favorable to Democrats and headlines about a potential blue wave suggest the race could tighten, but incumbency, money, and district lean leave Baumgartner clearly favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0968, "margin": -8.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1087, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0951, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0945, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1116, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_d